# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written by AI agents, then checked
# in the rounds below, against the sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://archive.org/details/SwedishCourseText> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ,
                       <#review-7> ,
                       <#review-8> ,
                       <#review-9> ,
                       <#review-10> ,
                       <#review-11> ,
                       <#review-12> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The course was planned and written by AI agents working for Anton Wiklund, from an outline of all ten chapters (issue #884): chapters 0 to 2 first, then chapters 3 to 9 by four agents working in parallel from one brief, the Swedish and English first and the German, Spanish, French and Korean translated from the English, and checked by AI agents and by machines (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, and a second SHACL validator); no person has reviewed it."@en ,
                 "Selection: one story over nearly a year in Sweden with the same people, Sam, newly arrived in Uppsala from Canada, and Elin, a neighbor, with Sam's colleagues Karin and Johan, Elin's parents Anders and Lena and her friend Nora, so that the places, phrases and grammar come back from chapter to chapter: a first fika, shopping for dinner, the shared laundry room, a first day at work, picking mushrooms in the woods, the health center, Lucia and Christmas, a weekend in Dalarna, planning midsummer and midsummer itself, from August to June. Sam's gender is never stated; in Swedish Sam is hen. The people and places are invented, apart from the cities and regions. The level rises from beginner, about CEFR A1, after a few words such as those of Basic Swedish, to about B1. No card front repeats one of Basic Swedish; the questions ask what a line means, and practice in choosing forms such as en and ett is left to a deck of its own."@en ,
                 "Structure: 10 chapters of 30 steps in all, three per chapter: the scene, a dialogue with its key phrases explained; how it works, the chapter's grammar point; and another way it goes, a variant dialogue and a note on culture. Each step is read in two chunks and checks two or three questions met in its theory, and each chapter has two review questions, asked only in its final review: 87 step questions and 20 review questions, 107 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors), each with a note on why it is wrong. The front is Swedish, in today's standard spelling, as Swedes say it to each other, with du to everyone as Swedes do; the back, note and options are in English, German, Spanish, French and Korean, with du, tu and tú between Sam and the people Sam knows, Sie, usted and vous to and from staff and the doctor, and polite 해요체 in Korean. The theory is in English, German, Spanish, French and Korean (since version 2), with the Swedish dialogue lines the same in each."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0. Its dialogues, theory, questions, options and notes are the course's own text, written with the help of AI. The FSI course (a work of the United States Government) and Wikidata (CC0 1.0) allow any reuse, and English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to check meanings and genders only; no text was taken from any of the three."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 0 to 2: Swedish, English and questions (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Chapters 0 to 2 in Swedish and English before translation, read by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent: grammar, standard spelling and naturalness of every dialogue line and of the Swedish quoted in the notes, the facts in the theory, the English glosses and American spelling, the questions (one right option, plausible wrong ones, fronts met in their step's theory) and the story's continuity. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 23 findings, all applied before translation. Among them: the definite plural ending (äggen ends in -en, not -a), aldrig placed after tvätta, the helping verbs kan, ska, vill and måste named beside är as present tenses without -r, Första fikan for Första fikat, staff speakers named Baristan and Expediten, \"till\" replaced by checkout, Hur länge är det kvar? for a clumsy question, a review question whose line was missing from the dialogue, and three gaps in the story: gluten-free bread both on the shelf and sold out, Sam's laundry before Sam had a time, and Sam treating without ordering."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 0 to 2: translations (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The German, Spanish, French and Korean of the course's title, description, keywords, chapters and cards, each translated from the English by its own AI agent, with the wording of Korean at the Table as the model; each agent reported English it found ambiguous. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Two agents found that eight notes on wrong options said \"here the subject comes first\" about the card's front, which read as if they spoke of the wrong option; the eight notes now name the front's own words, in all five languages."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 0 to 2: answers in five languages and course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release in Turtle, read by an AI agent working independently of the compiling and translating agents: for each card in German, Spanish, French and Korean, whether the back means the Swedish front in its dialogue, whether each wrong option keeps its meaning and stays wrong, and whether the notes match the English; correct options that stand out by length or form; grammar, typography and naturalness in each language; and the course as a whole (story, facts taught twice, repeated fronts, glosses against backs, the description against the chapters, chapter titles in all six languages). Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: About 30 findings, all applied. Wrong options in Korean and French that differed from the back only by a question mark now differ by their form too (est-ce que, -나요); a German note no longer gives Sam a gender; the distant Korean 그쪽 between the two friends gave way to their names; splitting the bill, not the food, in German and French; and smaller fixes to wording, tense, typography and the Korean, Spanish and French descriptions. The story now says why Sam looks for gluten-free bread (Elin can't eat gluten). The description's counts and contents, the chapter titles in six languages, the timeline and the 31 distinct fronts were found to agree."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 0 to 2: sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The course's grammar points and key words against the three sources, searched by an AI agent with scripts on 18 November 2026: 18 grammar points and phrases in the OCR text of the FSI course, 36 key words in English Wiktionary's Swedish entries and in Wikidata's Swedish lexemes through the Wikibase API. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card. The FSI course has the present tense in -r, the verb in second place, questions with the verb first, inte after the verb, en and ett, the ending for the, five plural classes (veckor, middagar, gånger, äpplen, glas), mitt and ditt with ett words, ett stort glas and plural -a, the imperative, var as the past of vara, man, ingen, inget and inga, gärna, det var så lite as an answer to thanks, bjuda på, ifrån, and the spread of du. Wiktionary has Swedish entries for 33 of the 36 words, with the genders and meanings the course gives, and vad får det lov att vara? (what will it be, when taking an order); tvättid, kardemummabulle (which has since left the course) and barista have none. Wikidata has lexemes for 33 of the words and confirms the forms the course states (te neuter; lakan, lakanet, lakan; ägg, ägget, äggen; tomater; äpplen, äpplena), and items for fika (Q742793), påtår (Q10647938) and the du reform (Q690440, the 1960s and 1970s). Three points were changed after the check: the imperative is described as the present tense without its ending, as the FSI course has it, not as the stem; påtår is often free or cheap, after Wikidata; and en kaffe is explained as a cup ordered, since all three sources give kaffe as an ett word. No source covers tvättid, ingen fara, det gör inget, vad får det lov att vara outside Wiktionary, or fika as a noun outside Wiktionary."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 3 to 9: Swedish, English and questions (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Chapters 3 to 9, written by four AI agents in parallel from one brief, read before translation by two other AI agents working apart, one for chapters 3 to 6 and one for chapters 7 to 9 with the story across all ten chapters: grammar, standard spelling and naturalness of every dialogue line and of the Swedish in the notes, the facts and grammar in the theory, the English glosses, the questions, and the story's continuity. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 33 findings, all applied by the writing agents. Among them: a farewell that contradicted a plan made two lines earlier; får, unlike kan, vill, måste and ska, keeps its -r; the fourth verb group named strong and irregular; when Sam fell ill; gluten-free gingersnaps for Elin; Tack för att jag får komma on arriving and fick on leaving; superlatives in -st that take -a (den bästa); Sam tasting herring again, not for the first time; a look back that no longer says Sam understood almost nothing at the first fika; nearly a year from August to June; Om det regnar, äter vi jordgubbarna inne; and wrong options that were arguably right, such as Ta hand om dig for Krya på dig."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 3 to 9: translations and answers (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The German, Spanish, French and Korean of chapters 3 to 9 and of the course's description, each translated from the English by its own AI agent with the reviewed chapters 0 to 2 as the model, then read by two other AI agents working apart, each for its own chapters in all five languages: the backs against the Swedish fronts in their dialogues, wrong options that stay wrong and do not stand out, notes against the English, grammar and naturalness, the chapter titles and descriptions in six languages, and the description against the chapters. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: About 60 findings, all applied by the translating agents. Most were notes in German and French that gave Sam a gender (der Sprecher, un patient, celui qui parle) or made Nora, Karin and Elin male, now worded without one; the others: the doctor and the pharmacist given Sie, usted and vous throughout; Spain's vosotros between friends, as the Spanish's words are Spain's; Korean backs that could be read as their wrong options (뭐 했어요 and 뭐라도 했어요); a brother of no stated age (남자 형제); set phrases as the wrong options of Krya på dig; Da stimme ich zu for Jag håller med, which names no one; and chapter descriptions that now say the friends plan how to celebrate midsummer and eat the semla back in Uppsala. A check by script then found no back or wrong option that differs from another only by punctuation, in any of the five languages."@en .

<#review-7>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters 3 to 9: sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The grammar points, phrases and facts of chapters 3 to 9 against the three sources, searched by an AI agent with scripts on 18 November 2026: 22 grammar points and phrases in the OCR text of the FSI course, 45 key words in English Wiktionary's Swedish entries and in Wikidata's Swedish lexemes, and Wikidata items for the customs and institutions the notes name. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card's Swedish. The FSI course has 21 of the 22 points: helping verbs with an infinitive, ska and kommer att (\"Kommer att is strictly a prognosis\"), halv fyra and kvart i fem, the four conjugations, the supine with har, tänker as plan to, som, sin, vilken, comparison (kall, kallare, kallast; bra, bättre, bäst), den stora boken, lägga sig and känna sig, inte before the verb in a subordinate clause, hade with the supine, hälsa på with stress on på, tycka and tro, tack för senast, tack för maten and skål, allemansrätten (\"Att plocka bär och svamp är tillåtet\"), Lucia on 13 December, julafton on 24 December and the maypole on midsommarafton; the 22nd, krya på dig, is not in it. Wiktionary has Swedish entries for 42 of the 45 words, with the course's meanings and genders, among them hen (a gender-neutral pronoun first proposed in 1966), lagom, bruka, semla eaten on Shrove Tuesday and midsommarafton as the Friday from 19 to 25 June. Wikidata has lexemes for 40 of the words and items for Saint Lucy's Day (13 December), the lussekatt, semla and Shrove Tuesday, Falu red, midsummer in Sweden, the maypole, Små grodorna, the right of public access and 1177. Four notes were changed after the check: the doctor now says a virus, not bacteria, instead of naming halsfluss, which can be either; majstång is no longer explained by a verb maja, which no source supports; hen is said to have been first proposed in the 1960s and to have come into wide use in the 2010s; and Donald Duck on Christmas Eve is said to be shown for decades rather than since 1960. No source covers Falu red coming from the Falun mine, the constitution's mention of the right of public access beyond Wiktionary, or sjukanmäla sig as a reflexive verb."@en .

<#review-8>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Whole course: story and recall (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All ten chapters in Swedish and English, read by three AI agents that had not seen the course before: one read the titles, descriptions and theory in order as one story (cohesion, contradictions in people, places and dates, character, everyday Swedish life, and the level rising); two judged every question twice, once as asked in its chapter right after the theory and once as a card recalled months later out of context (a front that means something on its own, a back that is a natural and complete answer, notes that say who says it to whom, wrong options that do not give the answer away). Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 46 findings, applied to the Swedish and English and then carried into the four other languages. The story: Sam's new job now starts two weeks after the first fika, as the chapters need; Elin orders a gluten-free cake, not a cardamom bun; Sam comes back for the laundry instead of being both gone and there; herring was tasted once, at the Christmas buffet, and the midsummer line now regrets not eating more of it; a line about Christmas food that answered nothing; no past tense before it is taught; mushrooms picked at the end of September, when chanterelles grow; reasons for the weekend in Dalarna; a guest cabin for two; the last chapter retitled The first midsummer; and the look-back now remembers the pink sheet too. The questions: backs that carry what the front says (i lördags as last Saturday; It doesn't matter for Det gör inget), fronts that no longer depend on a name or a place in the story, a back that gave itself away (pickled herring), a duplicate lesson replaced by one on vilka, wrong options that changed two things at once, and fewer wrong options that only turn the front into a question."@en .

<#review-9>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Whole release: final detailed review (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The release as it is published, in all six languages, read line by line by six AI agents that had not seen it before, five for two chapters each (the Swedish, the English, every card in all five answer languages, facts and grammar, card design, the story with the chapters around them, the Markdown) and one for the release's metadata and provenance (every count and every claim of the review records checked against the file). Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 30 findings, all applied: two must-fix (a French chapter description whose verb had no subject, and a Korean back that read like one of its own wrong options) and 28 others. Among them: wrong options that were arguably right (a rule for private land, and the right to roam, for allemansrätten; Are you taking anything for the fever?; then we'll tell them, for då säger vi så), an answer that stood out as the only one built with est-ce que or -나요, a party farewell that now has a reason (Karin leaves early), times written 11:30, glömmer → glöm with one m, frusen → frusna, hen's history, a note that misread a dialogue, and source records that still named words the course no longer uses. The counts in this release (10 chapters, 30 steps, 87 step and 20 review questions, 107 cards, 321 wrong options) were checked by script."@en .

<#review-10>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-18T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Whole release: by language and as a learner (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Six more AI agents that had not seen the release: five each read one language across all ten chapters, as a careful native editor would (Swedish, German, Spanish, French, Korean: the same terms and register throughout, grammar, typography and natural phrasing, backs that stand out and options that could be right), and one took the course as an English-speaking beginner, answering every question only from what the course had taught so far. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 64 findings, all applied, and one change of design. The learner could answer all 107 questions from the course alone, but on 47 cards one wrong option was the only question among statements, or the only statement among questions, and could be ruled out without reading the Swedish; on 35 of them it was replaced by a wrong option of the same kind (another person, tense, time, place or word), and it was kept on the 12 cards that teach word order. The other findings: two grammar steps split into more chunks, rules given before they are needed (short verbs in the imperative, inte in a question, de här strumporna, kunna after skulle), why Karin says hen, a herring line and an unidiomatic dialogue line in Swedish, notes in French that still gave Sam a gender, tú and tu to a stranger, Korean particles after Swedish words, and wording in all five answer languages. A script then confirmed that every front is in its dialogue, no two options of a card differ only by punctuation, and every Swedish quote in the notes is the same in all five languages."@en .

